  "Source": "N. J. Harrick and A. F. Turner, \"A Thin Film Optical Cavity to Induce Absorption or Thermal Emission,\" Appl. Opt. 9, 2111-2114 (1970). doi:10.1364/AO.9.002111 - Table IV (p design), p. 2113; construction of Fig. 1",
  "Notes": "Cavity inducing unit p-polarized thermal emittance Ep = 1 - Rp = 1 from a gold emitter at 5.5 um, viewed through a Si hemicylinder\nSpecification: Ep = 1 - Rp = 1 at 5.5 um, alpha = 28.8 deg in the Si hemicylinder.\nReported: Table IV title: calculated cavity data to induce Ep = 1 at 5.5 um (i.e. Rp = 0 at 28.8 deg). Text: cavities are first order; for the p component emission peaks (not shown) vs wavelength are more closely spaced, resolving power ~1 um; emittance of the other polarization is small.\nNotes: Table IV h/lambda at 5.5 um: cavity n1 = 3.1 0.052 -> 286 nm, FTR n2 = 1.45 0.290 -> 1595 nm. Reversed from the paper's emitter-first list. Gold at 5.5 um n0 = 9.05 - i28.2 (Table IV footnote, presumably ref. 8). Emittance is evaluated by Kirchhoff's law as E = 1 - R of the opaque gold-backed stack seen from the Si, with angle of emission = angle in the Si hemicylinder.\nLayers from Table IV (p design), p. 2113; construction of Fig. 1; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours Rp 0.000, Rs 0.998 at 28.8 deg, 5500 nm; paper Rp 0 (the design condition, Table IV).",
